"For a compact set $D\\subseteq\\mathbb{R}^2$, an integer $n\\ge\n3$, and points $p_1,\\dots,p_n\\in D$, let\n\n$$\nm(p_1,\\dots,p_n)=\\min_{1\\le i<j<k\\le n}\\operatorname{area}(p_ip_jp_k),\n$$\n\nwhere\n$\\operatorname{area}(p_ip_jp_k)$ denotes the area of the triangle with vertices $p_i,p_j,p_k$,\ntaken to be $0$ when the three points are collinear. Define\n\n$$\n\\Delta_D(n)=\\max_{p_1,\\dots,p_n\\in D} m(p_1,\\dots,p_n),\n$$\n\nthe maximum being attained because $D$ is compact and $m$ is continuous.\n\nIf $D$ and $D'$ are compact convex sets of nonzero area, then there are\nconstants $0<c\\le C$ depending on $D$ and $D'$ but not on $n$ such that\n\n$$\nc\\,\\Delta_{D'}(n)\\le\\Delta_D(n)\\le C\\,\\Delta_{D'}(n)\\qquad\\text{for all }n\\ge 3,\n$$\n\nsince some affine image of $D'$ is contained in $D$ and an affine map multiplies\nall triangle areas by a fixed factor. Consequently the growth rate of\n$\\Delta_D(n)$, up to constant factors, does not depend on the choice of compact convex $D$\nof nonzero area; write\n$\\Delta(n)$ for this common growth rate, and write $\\Delta_\\square(n)$ when\n$D$ is the unit square $[0,1]^2$.\n\nDetermine the asymptotic behaviour of $\\Delta(n)$ as $n\\to\\infty$: exhibit an\nexplicit function $f$ with $\\Delta(n)=\\Theta\\bigl(f(n)\\bigr)$. In particular,\ndetermine whether the limit\n\n$$\n\\alpha=\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\frac{\\log\\bigl(1/\\Delta_\\square(n)\\bigr)}{\\log n}\n$$\n\nexists, and determine its value, i.e. determine the least exponent\n$\\alpha$ such that $\\Delta(n)=n^{-\\alpha+o(1)}$."